package cajeta.hash;

/**
 * Hash utility namespace.
 *
 * The static methods here are the cajeta-side surface for the runtime
 * hash primitives:
 *
 * - **identity hashing** for `IdentityHashMap` / observer registries
 *   / weak-ref tables — anywhere *same heap object* is the equality
 *   notion, not *structurally equal*
 * - **manual combination** of two field hashes for hand-written
 *   `hash()` overrides
 * - **per-process random seed** access so external callers can align
 *   with the compiler-synthesized `Object.hash()`
 *
 * ## Sibling classes
 *
 * The full **hash algorithm** classes live alongside this file under
 * `runtime/src/cajeta/hash/` and follow the same pattern — cajeta-
 * source declarations with `@Native` methods bridging to the
 * corresponding `__cajeta_hash_*` runtime symbols in
 * `runtime/native/cajeta_runtime.c`:
 *
 * | Class           | Algorithm                | Use case                                |
 * |-----------------|--------------------------|-----------------------------------------|
 * | `XXHash3`       | XXH3-64                  | fast general-purpose; default backing   |
 * | `RapidHash`     | rapidhash                | maximum throughput, smaller code        |
 * | `SipHash`       | SipHash-2-4              | DoS-resistant, key-derived              |
 * | `MD5`           | MD5                      | checksum / identifier, **not security** |
 * | `DefaultHasher` | XXH3-64 + seed           | what `Object.hash()` uses               |
 *
 * **Cryptographic hashes** (SHA-2, SHA-3, BLAKE2/3) and AEAD /
 * signature / KDF primitives live in the future `cajeta.crypto` peer
 * library — never inside `cajeta.hash`.
 *
 * ## Usage
 *
 * All entry points are static — there is nothing to construct.
 *
 * ```cajeta
 * // Same-heap-object identity, for an `IdentityHashMap` key
 * stack int64 id = Hash.identity(obj);
 *
 * // Hand-written hash() override threading two field hashes through
 * // the same mixer the structural synthesizer emits
 * public int64 hash() {
 *     return Hash.combine(this.name.hash(), this.value.hash());
 * }
 *
 * // Process seed, e.g. to replay a hash-table snapshot elsewhere
 * stack int64 seed = Hash.processSeed();
 * ```
 *
 * @See cajeta.lang.Object
 * @See [specification/hash/Hashing.md](../../../../../docs/specification/hash/Hashing.md)
 */
public final class Hash {

    /**
     * Pointer-based identity hash.
     *
     * Returns `obj`'s pointer bit pattern mixed through the SplitMix64
     * finalizer with the per-process seed. Used wherever *same heap
     * object* is the desired equality — `IdentityHashMap`, observer
     * registries, weak-ref tables, debugger pinning sets — instead of
     * *structurally equal* (the default `Object.hash()` shape).
     *
     * Parameter type is the universal root `cajeta.lang.Object`. Every
     * class implicitly extends `Object`, so any class reference passes
     * here without an explicit cast. The underlying runtime symbol
     * still takes a raw `void*`; the ABI is identical — only the
     * cajeta-side type widens for static type checking.
     *
     * ```cajeta
     * stack int64 id = Hash.identity(obj);   // null-safe, O(1)
     * ```
     *
     * @Param obj the object to hash; `null` is a valid input and
     *            hashes to the seed-mixed bit pattern of the zero
     *            pointer.
     * @returns 64-bit identity hash of `obj`.
     * @Complexity O(1).
     */
    @Native("__cajeta_hash_identity")
    public static int64 identity(Object obj);

    /**
     * Combines two 64-bit hash values into one with good distribution.
     *
     * Implementation pattern is Boost's `hash_combine` with the
     * SplitMix64 finalizer appended — well-known mixer, no surprises
     * around adversarial inputs (the per-process seed handles
     * collision-flooding defense; `combine` itself is deterministic).
     *
     * Used by **manual `hash()` overrides** that thread multiple
     * field hashes together when the structural-synthesis default
     * isn't the right shape. The synthesizer itself emits this exact
     * call between consecutive field hashes, so manual overrides and
     * synthesized bodies produce identical hashes for identical field
     * sets in identical order.
     *
     * ```cajeta
     * stack int64 h = Hash.combine(first.hash(), second.hash());
     * // order-sensitive: Hash.combine(a, b) != Hash.combine(b, a)
     * ```
     *
     * @Param a first hash input.
     * @Param b second hash input.
     * @returns combined hash. **Order-sensitive**:
     *          `combine(a, b) != combine(b, a)` in general.
     * @Complexity O(1).
     */
    @Native("__cajeta_hash_combine")
    public static int64 combine(int64 a, int64 b);

    /**
     * The process-wide random seed.
     *
     * Initialized from the OS cryptographic-entropy syscall at
     * process start via a constructor function in the runtime:
     * `getrandom(2)` on Linux (kernel 3.17+; safe inside chroots /
     * containers because no file descriptor is required),
     * `getentropy(3)` on macOS / BSD, `BCryptGenRandom` on Windows.
     * Stable for the process lifetime; differs across restarts.
     *
     * **No fallback on entropy failure.** If the syscall fails (very
     * rare; usually means the system is mid-boot or extremely
     * degraded), the runtime logs and aborts with
     * `CAJETA_ERROR_ENTROPY_UNAVAILABLE`. Silent degradation to
     * weak entropy would defeat the entire HashDoS-protection story
     * — the user would think they're protected when they aren't.
     * The earlier doc text mentioning a `clock + pid` fallback was a
     * design error, removed 2026-05-18.
     *
     * The seed is **mixed into every hash this namespace produces** —
     * `identity`, `combine`, the primitive specializations, the
     * SipHash byte path. That kills hash-flooding attacks: an
     * attacker can't precompute keys whose hashes collide for
     * *your* process because they don't know your seed.
     *
     * Exposed here for direct callers that need to align with the
     * compiler-synthesized `Object.hash()` — e.g. external hash-table
     * snapshot replay where the seed must be threaded through and
     * pinned to reproduce an in-process state on a separate machine.
     *
     * ```cajeta
     * stack int64 seed = Hash.processSeed();   // stable per process, never zero
     * ```
     *
     * @returns the process-wide seed; never zero (the OS entropy
     *          syscall is itself contractually non-zero).
     * @Complexity O(1).
     */
    @Native("__cajeta_hash_seed")
    public static int64 processSeed();
}
